# std::comptime — compile-time facilities the compiler supplies at the call site.
#
# `embed` and `embed_dir` bake file bytes INTO the compiled binary at compile time: the compiler
# reads the named path(s) while it compiles and materializes the bytes as the value, so the program
# carries its own copy with nothing left to read at run time. cf uses them to host its own standard
# library — a shipped cf resolves `std::…` imports with no lib/std on disk — but a program can embed
# any asset the same way: a template, a shader, a lookup table.
#
# Both are pure comptime. The path must be a compile-time string LITERAL (the compiler reads it
# then, not at run time); the result is baked data, not an allocation, so an `embed` is colorless
# exactly like a string literal and carries no `!`. Paths resolve relative to the compiler's
# working directory at build time. A path that cannot be read is a compile error.

# One embedded file: its `path` (as encountered by the directory walk, from the given root) and its
# `content` (the file's raw bytes, baked in).
pub data EmbeddedFile = { Str path, Str content }

# The bytes of the file at `path`, read at compile time and baked into the binary as a static `Str`.
pub intrinsic embed = (Str path): Str

# Every file under `dir`, walked recursively, each as an `EmbeddedFile` whose `content` is the
# file's baked bytes. The list follows the directory walk order.
pub intrinsic embed_dir = (Str dir): [EmbeddedFile]
